---
title: Green salad with vinaigrette
description: Lesson 4.2. Making oil and vinegar stay together.
servings: 2
time: 10 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, eggs-and-emulsions]
lesson: "4.2"
technique: emulsifying
status: draft
---

> Technique: emulsifying. Oil and vinegar do not mix, but one can be
> broken into droplets small enough to hang in the other for a while.
> A vinaigrette is that, plus something to keep the droplets apart.

In a #bowl{}, whisk @red wine vinegar{2%tbsp} with @Dijon mustard{1%tsp} and @salt{1/4%tsp} until the salt has dissolved.

> Why mustard: it is an emulsifier. Its molecules hold on to water or
> vinegar at one end and to oil at the other, so they sit on the
> surface of the droplets and keep them from merging. The lecithin in
> egg yolk does the same job.
-- source: Kate Bernot, "The Best Way to Whisk, According to Science", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3417-the-best-way-to-whisk-according-to-science ; Andrew Janjigian and Rebecca Hays, "Finally, Homemade Dressing That Won't Separate or Solidify", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-05-16, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/169-make-ahead-vinaigrette

Pour in @olive oil{6%tbsp} in a thin stream while whisking hard from side to side, until the dressing is thick, cloudy and all one colour.

> Why three to one: three parts oil to one part vinegar is the usual
> starting ratio, not a law. Taste the dressing on a leaf. If it is
> sharp, whisk in a little more oil; if it is flat, a few drops more
> vinegar or a pinch of salt.

> Why side to side: in the test kitchen's trials, whisking side to side
> emulsified a vinaigrette better than stirring in circles. It breaks
> the oil into smaller drops.

> Science: an emulsion is one liquid dispersed as droplets in another.
> The smaller and more even the droplets, the longer it lasts. It can
> fail in several ways: creaming, where droplets float up; flocculation,
> where they clump; and coalescence, where they merge back into a layer
> of oil. A plain vinaigrette typically holds for no more than an hour.
> Look up: emulsion, emulsifier, coalescence, flocculation.
-- source: McClements, "Critical review of techniques and methodologies for characterization of emulsion stability", Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition 47(7):611-649, 2007, https://doi.org/10.1080/10408390701289292 ; Janjigian and Hays (as above, "no more than an hour")

Put @salad leaves{150%g}(washed and dried) in a #large bowl{}. Whisk the dressing once more, pour over just enough to coat, toss, and serve at once.

> Next time, without the recipe: acid, salt and an emulsifier first,
> then oil in a thin stream with hard whisking. Start at three to one
> and adjust by taste.
